.00818634033203125, -0.04150390625, 0.00637054443359375, -0.0206146240234375, -0.00018286705017089844, -0.0287628173828125, -0.0024509429931640625, 0.0215606689453125, 0.0142059326171875, -0.0016450881958007812, -0.00746917724609375, 0.01390838623046875, 0.01702880859375, -0.00518798828125, -0.05157470703125, -0.0051727294921875, -0.0178985595703125, 0.008392333984375, 0.0076446533203125, 0.01071929931640625, 0.0198822021484375, -0.0156097412109375, 0.01446533203125, -0.007038116455078125, -0.0172882080078125, -0.0126190185546875, -0.016815185546875, -0.040283203125, 0.0043182373046875, 0.00920867919921875, -0.01009368896484375, 0.006641387939453125, 0.0074462890625, -0.01910400390625, -0.00246429443359375, -0.0202789306640625, -0.0172576904296875, -0.03204345703125, 0.0153656005859375, 0.0168609619140625, -0.0255126953125, 0.006572723388671875, 0.0228118896484375, -0.018585205078125, 0.03485107421875, 0.0053863525390625, -0.00901031494140625, 0.012176513671875, -0.00043845176696777344, 0.02069091796875, -0.01287078857421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is ubiquitous in oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. However, reliably identifying and tracking these patterns, especially during transient dynamics, remains challenging. Here, we present a novel framework based on spatial ordinal patterns for characterizing the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. Unlike traditional phase-based analyses and standard permutation entropy, our approach acts directly on the phase of oscillators and introduces specific symbolic patterns to account for instances where neighboring phases are nearly equal, assigning a dedicated symbol to such configurations in the local spatial ordering.\n\nThis symbolic representation captures both phase gradients and synchronized clusters within a single, unified description by encoding local spatial relations of phases among neighboring oscillators. From this local construction, we define a spatial permutation entropy that quantifies the diversity of these spatial patterns across the network at each time point, thereby providing a time-resolved, network-level measure. By computing entropy either locally in neighborhoods or globally across the network, our method flexibly characterizes the spatial scale of pattern diversity.\n\nWe validate our framework using synthetic networks of coupled oscillators\u2014such as ring and grid topologies with varying coupling strengths and noise levels\u2014across synchrony, chimera, and traveling wave regimes. The approach is sensitive to regime transitions, which manifest as abrupt changes in spatial permutation entropy, enabling clear detection of transient dynamics. As an illustrative example, transitions from coherent waves to clustered states are marked by quantifiable shifts in the entropy time series that precede or coincide with underlying dynamical changes, a sensitivity that exceeds that of conventional phase coherence measurements.\n\nThe ability to distinguish phase-locked states with identical global synchrony but different spatial arrangements is enabled by direct examination of the symbolic pattern distributions and their associated entropy. We provide quantitative classification by thresholding entropy values and also visualize transitions using space-time plots of symbolic labels. \n\nWe further demonstrate the method on resting-state EEG recordings from human volunteers, comparing eyes-open and eyes-closed conditions within individuals. Here, the spatial permutation entropy shows consistent and condition-specific differences, revealing changes in the diversity of spatial phase relations; accuracy and sensitivity are assessed by comparing within-subject entropy distributions across conditions and computing classification metrics. This highlights the method\u2019s ability to detect subtle functional changes not apparent in standard EEG synchrony analyses.\n\nOur results demonstrate that spatial permutation entropy based on extended spatial ordinal patterns provides a unique and sensitive measure to track spatiotemporal organization and regime transitions in both model and empirical oscillatory systems."}}